    "lawCode": "Amd §2530, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6656\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to clarifying that\nthe nurse-family partnership program is not home care\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo clarify that the nurse-family partnership program is not home care\nunder Article 36 of the Public Health Law.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 3 of section 2530 of the Public Health\nLaw to make clear that the nurse-family partnership program shall not\nbe subject to Article 36 of the public health law.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nNurse-Family Partnership (NFP) is a voluntary nurse home visiting\nprogram for at-risk first-time mothers that is based on decades of\nresearch and adheres to an established set of program components and\npractices. The program produces statistically significant, positive\nprogram effects that are repeated and enduring for up to 15 years\nfollowing the child's birth.\n\nAlthough NFP services are generally provided in a participant's home,\nthe services do not constitute the services nor meet the kinds of\nneeds that Article 36 applies to. The legislative intent of Art. 36 is\nthat it apply to \"a coordinated array of services to patients in their\nhomes, thereby avoiding prolonged institutionalization.\" That is not\nthe NFP program. (PHL § 3600.)\n\nExpectant mothers are referred to NFP for guidance and education\nthroughout pregnancy, not for assistance with activities of daily\nliving or treatment of an illness or injury. NFP addresses the social\ndeterminants of health, including education, financial stability,\nemployment, housing, and health counseling. The education and guidance\nprovided by NFP nurses is intended to prevent or reduce unhealthy\nbehaviors that lead to medical conditions for the mother and/or child\nby empowering women to take ownership of their own decisions and\nbehaviors. If an NFP client develops medical conditions that require\nmedical care, then she is referred to an appropriate practitioner.\n\nFor 14 years, NFP was not regulated as a home care service program\nunder Article 36. However, some have incorrectly interpreted Article\n36 as requiring a medical order for a participant to receive NFP\nservices. This creates unnecessary and uncalled for delay and cost.\nConfirming the distinction between NFP and home care, and making it\neven clearer that Article 36 does not apply to the NFP program, is\nvital to the continued success of the program.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
